name bvr-intercept-1
timeout 300

# THE ONE-SIDED INTERCEPT: the pilot flies the whole beyond-visual-range sequence itself. Nothing about
# the shot is briefed — there is no `brief_release_s` line anywhere in this file, and that is the point.
# What is briefed is what a controller would give a fighter and nothing more: a VECTOR (one waypoint —
# bearing, distance, altitude) and a master-arm call. Everything after that is systems/FBPilot's
# Intercept phase deciding for itself, through the avionics command bus, on what its own boxes report.
#
# THE CAST
#   viper   friendly, `set task intercept`, two AIM-120s, CRM search (the F-16 mode that finds
#           everything and locks NOTHING — modules/f16/FBF16Fcr), RWR powered, datalink OFF. The
#           datalink is off in both jets for the same reason bfm-basic.fbm turns it off: with the net up
#           the intercept would be flown on somebody else's picture, and the sensor limitation would be
#           a claim rather than a constraint.
#   bandit  hostile, radar OFF, no weapons, flying a straight westbound leg at constant speed. It never
#           looks, never shoots and never manoeuvres, so every number in the viper's telemetry is a
#           property of the viper's decisions.
#
# THE GEOMETRY: 99 km apart, head-on, co-altitude at 8,000 m, closing at roughly 680 m/s. That is well
# OUTSIDE the APG-68's 40 nm (74 km) search gate, so the run starts with a genuine search phase — the
# pilot flying the briefed vector with the antenna pointed at the briefed altitude band, seeing nothing.
#
# WHAT THE RUN HAS TO SHOW, in telemetry.csv's eng_* columns and in events.log:
#   eng_state    search -> closing -> attack -> support (-> defend/abort if it comes to that)
#   eng_detect_s the first firm contact, ~74 km, i.e. the moment the geometry allowed it and not before
#   eng_lock_s   the RADAR_DESIGNATE event: the pilot's own TMS-forward at its briefed lock range,
#                deliberately late, because a lock is a warning to the target
#   eng_shot_s / eng_shot_nm / eng_shot_rtr_nm  the trigger, and the launch zone it was pulled in — the
#                shot is taken at Rtr (the range the round arrives from even if he turns and runs), not
#                at Raero, which is a shot he could outrun
#   eng_support_f / eng_pitbull  whether the lock was held all the way to the seeker's own activation,
#                which is the difference between a launch and a kill
#   DETONATION   with the miss distance
#
# THE RUN ENDS AT THE HIT (~173.0 s, exit 0). It used to run to its timeout, because a target survived
# everything; since core/FBDamageModel exists, the first burst (0.6 m, dead centre) destroys every
# system the bandit has, its own mission monitor concludes FAIL ("combat ineffective") and the run is
# over — the shot was the point of the mission, and the mission now ends when it lands. Everything
# before that tick is unchanged, column for column, and the debrief is still read from the eng_*
# columns' LAST ROW. What the FIRST kill is NOT is a SUCCESS line for the shooter: the combined verdict
# is the killed unit's FAIL, because no mission format has an objective that says "kill him" yet.
# The aftermath a kill has — a wreck flying on until the ground takes it — is measured in
# damage-amraam.fbm, whose target carries no objectives and can therefore not end its own run.

unit viper
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 6.40000 8000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm                         # search, no auto-lock: the pilot decides when to lock
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 8.35000 8000 450             # the controller's vector: threat bearing 090, co-altitude

  objective kill unit bandit
unit bandit
  team hostile
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 7.70000 8000 270.0 400
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off                         # it does not look, and therefore never warns anyone
  set rwr off
  wp 46.90000 5.90000 8000 400             # a straight westbound leg through the engagement
